    "UniqueConceptKey": "D-262-var-parametric-confidence",
    "Stem": "Flash Capital's trading desk holds a $50 million portfolio. The daily return is normally distributed with a mean of zero and a standard deviation of 2%. Using the parametric VaR at the 95% confidence level (1.65 standard deviations), what is the daily Value at Risk?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "$825,000",
      "B": "$1,000,000",
      "C": "$1,650,000",
      "D": "$2,500,000"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Parametric VaR = z-score x standard deviation x portfolio value. At 95% confidence, z = 1.65. VaR = 1.65 x 0.02 x $50,000,000 = $1,650,000. This is the amount the portfolio could lose at the 95% confidence level over one day under normal-distribution assumptions. Recomputed: 1.65 x 0.02 = 0.033; 0.033 x 50,000,000 = 1,650,000. Option C states the correct answer.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Option A is wrong because $825,000 applies the z-multiplier to only half the portfolio: 1.65 x 0.02 x $25,000,000. The parametric VaR applies to the full $50 million portfolio, giving 1.65 x 0.02 x $50M = $1,650,000, not $825,000.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B is wrong because $1,000,000 uses only one standard deviation (2% x $50M), ignoring the 1.65 multiplier for the 95% confidence level. One sigma covers about 68% of outcomes, not the 95% confidence VaR requires.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Option D is wrong because $2,500,000 uses 5% (the tail probability) rather than 1.65 standard deviations — a unit error: 0.05 x $50M. The correct computation multiplies the standard deviation (2%) by the confidence multiplier (1.65) before applying it to the portfolio value.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Apply",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "LOSTag": "D.2",
    "BlueprintDomain": "Risk Management",
